# 14. MANDATORY 20-POINT WEBSITE SECURITY PROGRAM

Implement all 20 controls across the frontend, backend, AI agents, database, research pipeline, automation workers, notifications, and deployment infrastructure.

These are actual engineering requirements, not merely checklist items.

## CHECK 1 — HIDE API KEYS

Keep Gemini keys, database credentials, service-role keys, signing secrets, and other private credentials server-side.

Never expose them in frontend bundles, public environment variables, client responses, source maps, or logs.

Use protected environment variables or a secret manager.

## CHECK 2 — ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE SECURITY

Separate development, testing, staging, and production configurations.

Provide a sanitized `.env.example`.

Ignore `.env` files, credentials, private keys, and sensitive configuration in Git.

Validate required configuration at startup and fail safely when critical production secrets are missing.

## CHECK 3 — GIT HISTORY AND SECRET SCANNING

Scan tracked files and Git history for credentials and tokens.

Integrate Gitleaks or an equivalent secret scanner into CI.

Document credential revocation and rotation procedures.

Remember that deleting a secret from the latest commit does not remove it from Git history.

## CHECK 4 — PROTECT ADMINISTRATIVE ROUTES

Enforce server-side authentication and authorization for all administrative pages and endpoints.

Protect research management, automation administration, security findings, audit logs, and user management.

Never rely solely on hidden frontend routes or client-supplied role values.

Test unauthorized access directly against backend endpoints.

## CHECK 5 — ROBUST AUTHENTICATION

Implement secure sign-up, sign-in, sign-out, password reset, session expiration, and session revocation where supported.

Validate JWT signatures, issuer, audience, and expiration when applicable.

Use a supported authentication provider and protect against brute-force attempts.

Never store plaintext passwords or expose privileged tokens in browser storage.

## CHECK 6 — ROLE-BASED ACCESS CONTROL

Define least-privilege roles for users, research reviewers, and administrators.

Validate record ownership for conversations, journals, moods, notifications, and automations.

Prevent unauthorized role changes and cross-user access.

Enforce permissions at both the backend and database layers where applicable.

## CHECK 7 — INPUT VALIDATION

Validate all request bodies, parameters, uploaded metadata, automation definitions, and external provider responses.

Use strict schemas, length limits, payload limits, and allowlists.

Validate external URLs to reduce SSRF risks.

Do not rely on sanitization alone to prevent injection attacks.

## CHECK 8 — CROSS-SITE SCRIPTING PROTECTION

Use safe rendering and context-aware output encoding.

Sanitize rendered Markdown with a maintained allowlist-based sanitizer.

Never execute user-generated content, research documents, or AI output as HTML or JavaScript.

Block dangerous URL schemes and unsafe DOM injection.

Test chat messages, journals, research metadata, citations, and admin interfaces against XSS payloads.

## CHECK 9 — SQL INJECTION PROTECTION

Use parameterized queries and safe ORM operations.

Never concatenate untrusted values into SQL statements.

Allowlist dynamic sorting and filtering parameters.

Apply least-privilege database credentials.

Test database search, filtering, authentication, administrative operations, and pgvector queries.

## CHECK 10 — DATABASE SECURITY AND RLS

Enable and test Row Level Security where supported.

Define policies for every table containing private or user-owned data.

Restrict research administration, safety records, audit logs, and system configuration.

Never expose service-role credentials to the frontend.

Test unauthorized inserts, reads, updates, and deletions.

## CHECK 11 — RATE LIMITING

Implement configurable per-user and per-IP rate limits for authentication, chat, uploads, search, automation endpoints, and administrative operations.

Apply stricter limits to expensive AI calls.

Use request timeouts, concurrency limits, payload-size limits, and bounded retries.

Use a shared rate-limit store when deploying multiple backend instances.

## CHECK 12 — CLOUD AND AI SPENDING CONTROLS

Configure application-level daily and monthly quotas, token limits, document-processing limits, concurrency limits, and notification limits.

Set provider budget alerts and spending limits where supported.

Monitor AI usage and estimated cost.

Do not claim external spending caps are active unless they have been configured and verified.

Fail safely when quotas are exhausted.

## CHECK 13 — SECURE FILE UPLOADS

Validate file content, extensions, MIME types, size, filenames, and storage destinations.

Allow only explicitly supported research formats, such as PDF, within configured limits.

Use private storage and safe generated filenames.

Prevent path traversal, executable uploads, and resource-exhaustion attacks.

Use malware scanning where available.

Isolate document extraction and treat uploaded documents as untrusted input.

## CHECK 14 — CSRF PROTECTION

For cookie-authenticated state-changing requests, implement appropriate CSRF tokens and origin validation.

Use SameSite cookies as an additional defense.

Protect sensitive state-changing actions.

Test cross-origin requests.

Do not assume that CORS alone prevents CSRF. Apply CSRF mechanisms appropriate to the actual authentication architecture.

## CHECK 15 — CORS CONFIGURATION

Allow only explicitly trusted origins.

Separate development and production origin settings.

Never use unrestricted wildcard origins with credentialed requests.

Restrict methods and headers to those required.

Test preflight requests and reject unauthorized origins.

## CHECK 16 — HTTPS AND TLS

Require HTTPS in production.

Configure TLS at the hosting provider or reverse proxy.

Redirect HTTP to HTTPS where appropriate.

Enable HSTS only after verifying the HTTPS deployment.

Use secure database and external-service connections.

Never disable certificate verification in production.

## CHECK 17 — SECURITY HEADERS

Configure suitable production headers:

- Content-Security-Policy.
- Strict-Transport-Security.
- X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff.
- Referrer-Policy.
- Clickjacking protection through CSP frame-ancestors and/or X-Frame-Options.
- Restrictive Permissions-Policy.

Verify actual deployed HTTP responses rather than assuming the headers are active.

## CHECK 18 — SECURE COOKIES

Configure applicable authentication cookies with:

- HttpOnly.
- Secure in production.
- An appropriate SameSite policy.
- Restricted Domain and Path.
- Appropriate expiry.
- Session rotation and revocation where supported.

Use the authentication provider's documented session architecture.

Avoid unnecessary custom cookies and JavaScript-accessible session identifiers.

## CHECK 19 — PRODUCTION DEBUG SETTINGS

Disable debug mode and verbose errors in production.

Never expose stack traces, SQL statements, internal paths, credentials, or sensitive provider responses to clients.

Use safe error responses with request IDs.

Redact secrets and sensitive personal information from logs.

Disable development-only routes, profiling tools, and test endpoints where appropriate.

## CHECK 20 — PRODUCTION CONFIGURATION AUDIT

Audit:

- Environment separation.
- Secret storage and rotation.
- Authentication and sessions.
- Database permissions and RLS.
- CORS and CSRF.
- HTTPS and headers.
- File uploads and storage.
- AI permissions and spending controls.
- Rate limits and quotas.
- Background workers and scheduler.
- Backups and recovery.
- Dependency vulnerabilities.
- Logs and alerts.
- Data retention and deletion.
- Consent and privacy.
- Administrator access.
- Deployment settings.

Fail safely when critical security requirements are missing.

